  "intro": "For powers of sine and cosine: if one power is odd, peel off one factor and convert the rest with $\\sin^2+\\cos^2=1$, then substitute. For even powers use the half-angle identities $\\sin^2 x=\\frac{1-\\cos 2x}{2}$ and $\\cos^2 x=\\frac{1+\\cos 2x}{2}$. Trigonometric substitution handles roots: $\\sqrt{a^2-x^2}$ suggests $x=a\\sin\\theta$, $\\sqrt{a^2+x^2}$ suggests $x=a\\tan\\theta$, and $\\sqrt{x^2-a^2}$ suggests $x=a\\sec\\theta$.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int \\sin^3 x\\,dx$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "The power of sine is odd; split off one factor: $\\sin^3 x=\\sin x(1-\\cos^2 x)$.",
          "Let $u=\\cos x$, so $du=-\\sin x\\,dx$: the integral becomes $-\\int(1-u^2)\\,du$.",
          "Integrate: $-\\left(u-\\frac{u^3}{3}\\right)+C$.",
          "Back-substitute: $-\\cos x+\\frac{\\cos^3 x}{3}+C$."
        ],
        "answer": "$-\\cos x+\\dfrac{\\cos^3 x}{3}+C$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int \\cos^2 x\\,dx$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Use the half-angle identity $\\cos^2 x=\\frac{1+\\cos 2x}{2}$.",
          "$\\int\\frac{1+\\cos 2x}{2}\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\int(1+\\cos 2x)\\,dx$.",
          "Integrate: $\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x+\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2}\\right)+C=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\sin 2x}{4}+C$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\dfrac{x}{2}+\\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{4}+C$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Set up $\\int \\dfrac{dx}{\\sqrt{9-x^2}}$ with a trig substitution and evaluate.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "The form $\\sqrt{9-x^2}$ suggests $x=3\\sin\\theta$, $dx=3\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta$.",
          "Then $\\sqrt{9-x^2}=3\\cos\\theta$, so the integral is $\\int\\frac{3\\cos\\theta}{3\\cos\\theta}\\,d\\theta=\\int d\\theta$.",
          "This gives $\\theta+C=\\arcsin\\frac{x}{3}+C$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\arcsin\\dfrac{x}{3}+C$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 4,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int \\sin^2 x\\,dx$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$\\sin^2 x=\\frac{1-\\cos 2x}{2}$; integrate to $\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\sin 2x}{4}+C$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\dfrac{x}{2}-\\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{4}+C$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 5,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int \\cos^3 x\\,dx$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$\\cos^3 x=\\cos x(1-\\sin^2 x)$; $u=\\sin x$: $\\sin x-\\frac{\\sin^3 x}{3}+C$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\sin x-\\dfrac{\\sin^3 x}{3}+C$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 6,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int \\dfrac{dx}{\\sqrt{4-x^2}}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$x=2\\sin\\theta$; integral becomes $\\int d\\theta=\\arcsin\\frac{x}{2}+C$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\arcsin\\dfrac{x}{2}+C$"
